WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:09.597
Picture the box office computer restarting ten minutes before the doors open, with the queue already out on the street.

2
00:00:09.597 --> 00:00:13.828
The program has to be back before the first customer reaches the counter.

3
00:00:14.733 --> 00:00:18.500
And every start repeats the whole of the last two lessons.

4
00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:19.604
Scan the classes.

5
00:00:19.604 --> 00:00:21.032
Read every annotation.

6
00:00:21.032 --> 00:00:23.240
Check the graph of who needs whom.

7
00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:25.903
And have Byte Buddy sew its costume copy.

8
00:00:26.833 --> 00:00:29.742
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

9
00:00:29.742 --> 00:00:34.041
One call through a stand-in costs a few hundred nanoseconds at most.

10
00:00:34.041 --> 00:00:34.989
That's nothing.

11
00:00:34.989 --> 00:00:36.823
So aren't the stand-ins free?

12
00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:41.042
Let's be fair to that thought; it's half right.

13
00:00:41.042 --> 00:00:47.594
The lesson timed one call with JMH, a harness that measures code only after the JVM has warmed up.

14
00:00:47.594 --> 00:00:53.344
On a busy machine the times jumped around, so look at the bytes each call left behind.

15
00:00:53.567 --> 00:00:55.778
A direct call: zero bytes.

16
00:00:55.778 --> 00:00:58.586
Byte Buddy's subclass: about 160.

17
00:00:58.586 --> 00:01:00.202
The JDK proxy: 208.

18
00:01:00.202 --> 00:01:09.645
At 2,000 booking calls a second, that's 400 kilobytes a second, next to a network call that takes milliseconds.

19
00:01:09.645 --> 00:01:10.325
Nothing.

20
00:01:11.233 --> 00:01:13.629
So per call, the stand-ins are cheap.

21
00:01:13.629 --> 00:01:16.089
But there's a second clock: the start.

22
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:27.122
The demo starts the box office in a fresh JVM, wired by our container: fourteen parts, one JDK proxy, and one Byte Buddy subclass.

23
00:01:27.122 --> 00:01:32.339
How many classes does the JVM load before the first ticket is sold?

24
00:01:32.339 --> 00:01:33.352
About thirty?

25
00:01:33.352 --> 00:01:34.675
About a thousand?

26
00:01:34.675 --> 00:01:36.466
More than two thousand?

27
00:01:36.466 --> 00:01:37.712
Pause and guess.

28
00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:42.039
2,207.

29
00:01:42.039 --> 00:01:47.366
The same box office wired by plain code loads 991.

30
00:01:47.366 --> 00:01:50.668
So 1,216 classes are machinery.

31
00:01:50.668 --> 00:01:53.651
Here's where they come from.

32
00:01:54.567 --> 00:01:57.608
814 come from the Byte Buddy library itself.

33
00:01:57.608 --> 00:02:03.690
It loads its own model of types, and its class writer, before it can write one subclass.

34
00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:08.667
The costume department unpacks its whole sewing room to sew one costume.

35
00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:14.624
3 classes are what it sewed: the costume copy of the show closer, and two little helpers.

36
00:02:14.833 --> 00:02:19.737
30 are JDK proxy classes, and only one of them is our booking stand-in.

37
00:02:19.737 --> 00:02:25.194
Every annotation you read through reflection is an object of a proxy class too.

38
00:02:25.194 --> 00:02:29.061
So asking, is this a Component, makes stand-ins as well.

39
00:02:29.267 --> 00:02:36.569
And more than a hundred are hidden classes, which the JVM makes for its own use, for lambdas and method handles.

40
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:46.810
Every one of those classes is found, read and checked again, like the corner shop from the Startup track that unpacks every box each morning.

41
00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:50.640
Then it runs slowly at first, because nothing is warm yet.

42
00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:55.592
In the lesson's run, from main to the first ticket took 1,029 milliseconds.

43
00:02:55.592 --> 00:03:00.214
The machine was busy, and times moved by a factor of two between runs.

44
00:03:00.214 --> 00:03:02.195
The class counts barely moved.

45
00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:04.532
So, is Byte Buddy slow?

46
00:03:04.532 --> 00:03:05.964
That's the wrong model.

47
00:03:05.964 --> 00:03:10.198
Byte Buddy does at every start what a compiler could have done once.

48
00:03:10.198 --> 00:03:17.420
Everything the container works out depends only on the classes, and the classes are fixed when the program is built.

49
00:03:18.333 --> 00:03:25.262
Picture the front-of-house manager from the first lesson, writing the evening's running order the afternoon before.

50
00:03:25.262 --> 00:03:27.732
On the night, nobody reads a single card.

51
00:03:27.732 --> 00:03:29.660
The crew just follows the sheet.

52
00:03:30.667 --> 00:03:31.646
Let's map that.

53
00:03:31.646 --> 00:03:33.407
The afternoon is the build.

54
00:03:33.407 --> 00:03:39.411
The running order is a Java class that a generator writes, a registry, with plain new calls.

55
00:03:39.411 --> 00:03:43.131
And the night is the start, which only follows the sheet.

56
00:03:44.033 --> 00:03:48.947
Each shared part gets a holder class: a small class with one static field.

57
00:03:48.947 --> 00:03:53.329
The JVM fills that field the first time someone touches the class.

58
00:03:53.329 --> 00:03:57.976
Once, even when many threads ask, under the class initialization lock.

59
00:03:57.976 --> 00:04:01.030
No map, no lock of our own, and no reflection.

60
00:04:01.030 --> 00:04:03.885
In Kotlin, an object is a holder class too.

61
00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:10.193
Now the same box office, started from the generated registry.

62
00:04:10.193 --> 00:04:11.254
991 classes.

63
00:04:11.254 --> 00:04:17.088
Nothing from Byte Buddy, no JDK proxies, nothing sewn at start-up.

64
00:04:17.088 --> 00:04:23.365
And ready in 266 milliseconds, against 1,029, on the same busy machine.

65
00:04:24.267 --> 00:04:26.096
That's the rule of this lesson.

66
00:04:26.096 --> 00:04:30.403
What depends only on your classes can be worked out once, when you build.

67
00:04:30.403 --> 00:04:33.353
Do it there, and leave the start only what's left.

68
00:04:34.433 --> 00:04:35.914
You give up two things.

69
00:04:35.914 --> 00:04:44.477
The set of parts is fixed when you build: nothing can pick a part from a setting at start-up, unless the generator wrote code for it.

70
00:04:44.477 --> 00:04:46.280
And a holder fails for good.

71
00:04:46.280 --> 00:04:52.718
If a constructor throws inside one, every later request throws NoClassDefFoundError until a restart.

72
00:04:52.718 --> 00:04:57.612
That's where our picture breaks: a manager on the night can still improvise.

73
00:04:57.612 --> 00:04:59.028
A printed sheet can't.

74
00:04:59.933 --> 00:05:01.726
So, are the stand-ins free?

75
00:05:01.726 --> 00:05:02.855
Per call, nearly.

76
00:05:02.855 --> 00:05:09.162
Per start, no: the machinery behind them loaded more than a thousand extra classes, every time.

77
00:05:09.162 --> 00:05:10.489
Measure both clocks.

78
00:05:10.489 --> 00:05:16.265
Then move what depends only on your classes to the build, and let the queue in on time.

79
00:05:16.467 --> 00:05:19.974
Our generator was a small program we ran by hand.

80
00:05:19.974 --> 00:05:27.061
In Part 5, the build itself reads your annotations and writes files like this every time, with KSP.

81
00:05:27.967 --> 00:05:28.711
Quick check.

82
00:05:28.711 --> 00:05:40.247
Which of these can the generated registry do when the program starts: pick the booking service from a command-line setting, or build the seating plan exactly once when eight threads ask?

83
00:05:43.433 --> 00:05:44.114
The second.

84
00:05:44.114 --> 00:05:48.010
The holder builds it once, under the class initialization lock.

85
00:05:48.010 --> 00:05:52.648
Picking a part from a setting needs code the generator wrote at build time.
